<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Saint Gall</h1><p class="st-meta">1206 CE – 1798 CE (approx.) · capital St. Gallen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Benedictine abbey famed for its early medieval library whose prince-abbots ruled north-eastern Switzerland until the French invasion of 1798.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The abbey grew from the cell of the Irish monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Gallus</a> and was founded as a monastery by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-othmar">Otmar</a> in 719. Its scriptorium, library and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-saint-gall">Plan of Saint Gall</a> made it a centre of Carolingian learning. Its abbots gained wide estates, and from 1206 they were recognised as princes of the Empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The prince-abbots governed lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thur">Thur</a>, but lost <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-appenzell">Appenzell</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appenzell-wars">Appenzell Wars</a> and clashed with the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a>. Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-rosch">Ulrich Rösch</a> rebuilt their power, buying the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-toggenburg">County of Toggenburg</a> in 1468 and allying with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy</a>. The abbey remained Catholic, while the city and parts of Toggenburg turned Protestant. Its baroque church and library were built in the 1750s and 1760s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The French invasion of 1798 and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> ended its secular rule, and the abbey was dissolved in 1805. Its lands became the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a> in 1803. The abbey precinct and library are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="prince bishopric, 1206 CE – 1798 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">prince bishopric</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.70%;width:11.78%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1206 CE – 1798 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>St. Gallen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Otmar, Ulrich Rösch, Beda Angehrn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prince-Abbey of St. Gallen, Fürstabtei St. Gallen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1502&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-saint-gall" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Saint Gall on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 515 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Saint Gall lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5219 771L5220 773M5219 771L5229 694M5219 771L5186 782M5219 771L5188 772M5219 771L5213 774"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5220 773h0M5229 694h0M5186 782h0M5188 772h0M5213 774h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5219 771h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5219 771h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1502&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-saint-gall">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
